## 2026-10-27 evidence table rename and current-set persistence

- Migration 200 renames `model_benchmark_search_evidence` to
  `model_benchmark_evidence`, removes `raw_json` for every source, and compacts
  rows by the exact existing formula-observation key. It keeps the latest
  `captured_at`; equal timestamps use the fitness reader's existing hash order.
  The migration runs in the existing migration transaction. Fresh and repeated
  initialization leave only the new table, with no alias or dual write.
- The replacement API handles exact `(modelId, source)` current sets, including
  valid empty sets. Duplicate scopes, mismatched row scopes, and duplicate
  incoming hashes reject before deletes. Plain inserts make conflicts roll back
  the transaction. Its synchronous callback publishes native fitness inside
  that same transaction.
- The full refresh validates all five current direct feeds before mutation,
  uses the complete fresh canonical models.dev catalog including inactive
  models, matches exact IDs before unique exact names, then replaces evidence
  and the four owned native score sources atomically. Missing canonical IDs
  lose all benchmark evidence and owned benchmark scores. Current IDs keep
  non-direct observations until a valid replacement for that source arrives.
  Successful HF and manual queued-source updates replace their exact sets;
  valid-empty updates clear them. Failed, malformed, timed-out, or incomplete
  responses preserve the last valid rows and scores. Malformed Ollama results
  use the existing 502 provider error path.
- Normal models.dev sync still writes pricing and capabilities, then starts an
  error-isolated refresh with the fresh full catalog. It does not enqueue or
  drain the separate manual discovery queue. In-process refresh calls serialize
  in invocation order while retaining each call's catalog input.
- The AutoCombo consumer regression proves raw canonical IDs reach native
  `model_intelligence` for both coding and general high-band filters; the
  provider-prefixed route ID remains separate. Formula, normalization,
  deduplication, effort profiles, thresholds, capability writes, Auto filters,
  and free-cost files are unchanged.
- Historical per-row data has no reliable source-batch or deletion boundary.
  Migration therefore compacts only exact duplicate formula keys, preserves
  distinct keys, and removes all raw payloads. Old deleted keys or successful
  empty outcomes cannot be reconstructed. For still-current catalog IDs,
  distinct non-direct observations remain last-known current cache until a
  valid replacement arrives; this is not an archive or a claim of recoverable
  source history.
- Review dispositions: the finding that older distinct keys may remain was
  checked against this accepted last-known-current contract; inferring omitted
  batch rows from the legacy schema would invent history. The normal-sync queue
  finding is closed by the explicit design: discovery queue work remains a
  separate manual path. No other actionable cross-module finding remains.
- Final offline verification: combined storage, loader/catalog, publisher,
  migration, search-handler, and queue suites pass 115/115; models.dev sync
  and hook suites pass 46/46; actual AutoCombo band consumer passes 5/5 using
  Vitest forks. The compiled operator bundle
  `dist/ops/reprocess-model-benchmark-search.mjs` has SHA-256
  `61ef7e74693af471d347708b6bb11e05e2b2ad227472ac13d142accc5737cde4`; its
  `--check-runtime` smoke loads the module graph in an isolated temp data
  directory. `npm run check:docs-all` and strict OpenSpec validation pass;
  docs checker reports 99 existing potential-drift warnings. Prettier and
  `git diff --check` pass.
- The ordinary `npm run build:fast` is blocked before compilation because this
  worktree's `node_modules` symlink resolves outside the filesystem root. The
  authoritative ARM64 image and upgraded-runtime migration rehearsal were not
  run here and remain release gates. After this fix merges, apply exact upstream
  v3.8.52 before any image build; rehearse migration 200 on an isolated
  consistent DB clone, verify schema/data/formula/FK/integrity semantics and
  restore the pre-migration snapshot with the prior image, then build one
  authoritative ARM64 image. No push, merge, deployment, production DB write,
  live search, or inference occurred in this local package.

